    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm truly concerned about your child's health and I understand your distress. However, it's extremely important not to administer a higher dose than prescribed. Albuterol, when taken in a concentrated form over an extended period, can be dangerous and is not advised by medical professionals for such scenarios.\n\nRather, encourage your child to remain seated and have them slowly breathe in through their mouth and out through their nose. Hold a gauze or cloth over their mouth to cover their nose if possible, while you help them breathe deeply in one, out one manner. This is to increase their oxygen intake, although without helping with lung capacity, which inhalers treat. Keep your child as calm and comfortable as possible; this can sometimes help alleviate breathing difficulties in the short term.\n\nI have to stress again that this is a very urgent situation and it still requires professional medical assistance. Keep calling for an ambulance or seek an urgent care facility if possible. I hope this is urgent care can get through soon. You're doing your best in a challenging situation, which is admirable. Stay strong until help arrives.",
